  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nToday-Sunday...The Atlantic ridge axis settles over the Florida\npeninsula today, where it is forecast to linger through the\nweekend. Aloft, mid and upper level ridging also meanders over the\nstate. Prior to sunrise this morning, showers and a few storms \nremain contained to the Atlantic waters along the Gulf Stream.\nExpect these cells to continue over the next several hours. A few\ncould brush the Treasure Coast this morning, before convection\ndevelops along the sea breeze later this morning and into the\nearly afternoon.\n\nOverall, little change day to day through Sunday, with light \nsoutherly flow increasing to 5-10 mph from the southeast as the \nsea breeze develops and moves inland in the afternoon. The sea \nbreeze will be the main focal point for convection each day, \nkicking off near the coast early in the afternoon. A collision is \nthen expected over the interior, near or west of the I-4 corridor,\ninto the evening hours. Slow storm motions will lead to erratic \nsubsequent propagation along outflow boundaries. Deep moisture\n(PWATs 2.1-2.4\") will support continued high coverage of showers\nand storms, with PoPs 50-70% today and 60-80% this weekend. Peak \ntiming for showers and storms each day looks to be 2-8 PM. A few \nstorms may be strong, with wind gusts near 50 mph, due to DCAPE \nvalues near 800-900 J/kg. However, the main threat looks to be \nlocally heavy rainfall of 1-3\" from slow moving thunderstorms. \nThis could lead to minor flooding of low- lying and poor drainage \nareas, especially at locations which see multiple rounds.\n\nWhile shower and storm coverage peaks through 8 PM, scattered\nshowers and a few storms (up to 30-40%) could linger each evening\nnear the sea breeze collision through around 10 PM before \ndiminishing. Otherwise, any nighttime showers and storms are\nforecast to remain mostly over the Atlantic waters. However, can't\nrule out a few to scattered cells along the Treasure Coast late\neach night into the early morning hours. High temperatures\ncontinue to reach the lower 90s, with high humidity producing heat\nindices 102-107 degrees. Overnight lows look to remain in the 70s.\n\nNext Week...Models are in good agreement that the Atlantic ridge\naxis will weaken early next week, as a strong ridge develops over\nthe eastern half of the US. With the ridge north of Florida,\nonshore flow (E/ENE) looks to develop, becoming breezy at times,\nespecially Wednesday. Available moisture looks to remain high,\nthough periods of drier air may advect into the area later in the\nweek. However, models suggest an upper level low could transit\naloft over the area during this time. Thus, scattered to numerous\n(50-70%) showers and storms are forecast to linger through next \nweek, with increasing coverage of onshore-moving convection, as \nopposed to the typical sea breeze regime. Seasonable highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s continue next week, though stronger onshore\nflow will keep overnight lows in the upper 70s along the coast.",
